          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A reaction is at equilibrium when the amounts of reactants or products no longer change. Chemical equilibrium is a dynamic process in which the rate of product formation by the forward reaction equals the rate of reactant formation by the reverse reaction. For instance, in a sealed bottle of carbonated water, the dissolved carbon dioxide reaches equilibrium with the gas above the liquid."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1731253444",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For any reaction at equilibrium, the reaction quotient "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " is equal to the equilibrium constant "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": ". If a reactant or product is a pure solid, pure liquid, or the solvent in a dilute solution, its concentration does not appear in the equilibrium constant expression. At equilibrium, the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant, although their specific values may vary depending on conditions. However, the reaction quotient will always equal "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": " ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "C"
              },
              {
                "text": " when using concentrations or "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "P"
              },
              {
                "text": " when using partial pressures). To determine if a reaction is at equilibrium, we compare the reaction quotient with the equilibrium constant."
              }
            ],
            "id": "hnd9lc88yrhgfg3",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
